WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:02.070
How many passwords do you have?

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.200
Ten, 20, 100?

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.559
Well, if you're like me, I've got 750 unique
passwords, strongly chosen,

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:12.910
and thankfully, I don't have to remember most
of them.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.780
I'll let you in on the secret.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.700
So how do most people solve that problem?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.350
It's a problem that was created by the security
department who had good intentions of making

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.220
you choose strong passwords.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.630
But strong passwords are generally hard to
remember.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.180
So what we end up with is this thing that
has been referred to as "PC sunflower".

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.820
It looks like this.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.010
It's a display monitor with a lot of little
yellow stickies all around it, each one of

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.120
them with the user ID and password.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.950
Obviously, not a very secure solution.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.530
What is a potentially other alternative that
people do?

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.952
Sometimes they take all those passwords, don't
put them on the monitor on the little yellow stickies.

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:57.079
Instead, they put them in an insecure file
like, say, a flat file, an Excel spreadsheet,

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:58.890
or even an email to themself.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.640
Also, not a good solution.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.331
Or, the third alternative most users choose
with-- left to their own devices is --is they

00:01:06.331 --> 00:01:08.430
set all the passwords to the same thing.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.790
Which again, not a good choice because if
you are able to figure out the password on

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.200
one system, you get it on all.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.290
The whole thing falls like a house of cards.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.189
So, what are the alternatives?

00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:23.900
Well, let's look at why we have this problem
in the first place.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:30.619
So we've got a user here who needs to log
in to, let's say, a number of different systems

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:32.020
down here.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.840
So this is system 1, system 2, system N.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:43.619
Now, this user needs to log in, so they're
having to log in here with password 1.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:46.009
They log in here with password 2.

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:49.549
They log in here with password N.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:55.910
And the guy is up here with this issue because
he can't remember all of these passwords.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.939
So that's why he went with those other solutions
that I was talking about, which are not secure.

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:03.829
They're not getting us to where we want to be.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.660
And the user has this conundrum of trying
to figure out how they can solve the problem.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.500
Now, what's a better alternative?

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:18.060
Well, let's put that user over here, and this
time we'll have him log in to his multiple

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:25.620
systems --system 1, 2 and N. Except in
this case, he's going to not log directly

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:31.860
into the system, he's going to log into a
thing I'm going to call a single sign-on manager.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.920
This is a special tool, software usually,
that's designed so that you log into this

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.290
with a single password that you've chosen
that's complex, and then it remembers the

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.090
passwords for all of these other systems.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.430
So we have a unique password for each one
of these systems.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.920
So this would be password 1-- actually,
this would be password A --to get you into

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.400
the single sign on.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.569
This is password 2 and this is password
N. So now we have a case where the user has

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:08.400
to keep up with only one password, which is
good news for that user.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.040
Now they feel this way.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.920
One password to get into all of the systems.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.300
So that makes their user experience much better.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.560
Now, what's the problem with this?

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:23.000
Some people would argue-- security types often
do this --that now you've introduced a single

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.330
point of failure (SPoF).

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.629
In other words, if someone figures out this
password, they can log in here and therefore

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:31.310
get access to all the others.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.519
Well, I'm going to say, first of all, to the
people that expressed this objection, have

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:38.069
you ever met one of these guys?

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:40.780
Do you know how they're solving their problem?

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:41.780
Remember what I told you?

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.860
They're putting them in insecure places.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:52.140
Or, if they're not doing that, they're setting
password 1 to password 2 to password N.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.599
They're choosing a single password for everything
anyway.

00:03:55.599 --> 00:03:58.329
So, it's the same problem.

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:03.261
The difference is, if it's the same password
in this scenario, then knowing the password

00:04:03.261 --> 00:04:07.739
here means you know the password here and
here-- and they all fall.

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:13.349
In this case, if someone knows the password
here because they were able to hack that system,

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:17.870
they do not know what these passwords are
because they're unique.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:25.280
So what that does for us is lessens the attack
surface to a single system at a time.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.880
And if you say "But how do I protect this?"

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.880
Now we're going to do that with multi-factor
authentication.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.169
With this now, it's not a single password
that gets you in, it's also something you

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:39.370
have or something you know or something you
are.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.470
It's some combination of those things.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:47.910
So now I have a secure gateway into this system,
and there it unlocks all the rest.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:54.500
So with a solution-- with single sign-on --we
end up with some distinct benefits.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.200
One of those is we end up with better security.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.810
Better security for the reasons I just talked
about.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:05.130
That is, we don't have one system falls and
they all fall.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.130
Now we have one system falls and that's the
extent of the damage.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.810
We have a better savings, cost-wise.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:12.810
Why is that?

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.880
Well, it turns out if a user has only one
password to remember, they're much more likely

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.500
to remember it, therefore not forget it, 

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:24.220
therefore not need to call into the help desk
in order to get it reset.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.830
And the statistics tell us that the number
one call to most help desk is "reset my password".

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.350
Sometimes is as much as 50% of the calls into
the helpdesk.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:39.040
That means half of your helpdesk calls is just
supporting the password reset problem.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.970
We can lessen if we used a single sign-on solution.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:47.449
And then at the end of the day, we also get
an improved user experience.

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:50.300
This user has only one thing to remember.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.449
Therefore, they don't need the PC sunflower.

00:05:52.449 --> 00:05:54.600
They don't need the Excel side file.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.370
They don't need to set all their passwords
to the same thing and compromise security.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.430
So this is one of these rare situations that
we need to jump on because we actually get

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:07.840
to improve security, lower cost, and improve the user experience.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:08.840
Thanks for watching.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.880
